npm install mongoose
import mongoose from 'mongoose';
await mongoose.connect(process.env.MONGO_URI!, {
// pool + driver options pass through to the official driver
maxPoolSize: 50,
minPoolSize: 5,
appName: 'shop-api',
serverSelectionTimeoutMS: 5000,
});
// optional: strict query behaviour
mongoose.set('strictQuery', true); // ignore unknown filter fields silently? false = warn
mongoose.set('debug', process.env.NODE_ENV !== 'production'); // log every op
// graceful shutdown
process.on('SIGTERM', async () => { await mongoose.disconnect(); process.exit(0); });
mongoose is a singleton wrapper around MongoClient. Sharing connections + pool across the whole app is automatic.

import { Schema, model, Types } from 'mongoose';
const UserSchema = new Schema({
name: { type: String, required: true, trim: true, minlength: 1 },
email: {
type: String,
required: true,
unique: true,
lowercase: true,
match: /^[^@\s]+@[^@\s]+\.[^@\s]+$/,
},
age: { type: Number, min: 0, max: 150 },
role: { type: String, enum: ['user', 'admin', 'staff'], default: 'user' },
tags: { type: [String], default: [] },
address: {
city: String,
country: { type: String, default: 'UK' },
},
meta: { type: Schema.Types.Mixed, default: {} }, // any shape
refId: { type: Schema.Types.ObjectId, ref: 'Order' }, // for populate
}, {
timestamps: true, // adds createdAt + updatedAt
collection: 'users', // override default 'users'
optimisticConcurrency: true, // adds __v + checks on save
toJSON: { virtuals: true },
toObject: { virtuals: true },
});
UserSchema.index({ email: 1 }, { unique: true });
UserSchema.index({ 'address.country': 1 });
export const User = model('User', UserSchema);
Schema type shorthand: name: String is equivalent to name: { type: String }.

Built-in: required, min, max, minlength, maxlength, match, enum.
Custom synchronous:
age: {
type: Number,
validate: {
validator: (v: number) => v % 1 === 0,
message: (props) => `${props.value} is not an integer`,
}
}
Custom async:
email: {
type: String,
validate: {
validator: async function (v) {
const exists = await this.constructor.findOne({ email: v }).lean();
return !exists;
},
message: 'email already taken',
}
}
Run validators on update:
await User.updateOne({ _id }, { $set: { age: 999 } }, { runValidators: true });
By default validators run only on save(). Always pass runValidators: true when using updateOne / updateMany / findOneAndUpdate if you want validation enforced.

Hooks fire around save, validate, remove, init, updateOne, findOneAndUpdate, aggregate, and more.
import bcrypt from 'bcrypt';
UserSchema.pre('save', async function (next) {
if (this.isModified('password')) {
this.password = await bcrypt.hash(this.password, 12);
}
next();
});
UserSchema.post('save', function (doc) {
publishEvent({ type: 'user.saved', id: doc._id });
});
Document middleware (save, validate, remove, init) — this is the document.
Query middleware (find, findOneAndUpdate, etc.) — this is the query, not the doc:
UserSchema.pre('find', function () {
this.where({ deletedAt: { $exists: false } }); // soft-delete filter on every find
});
Aggregation middleware:
UserSchema.pre('aggregate', function () {
this.pipeline().unshift({ $match: { deletedAt: { $exists: false } } });
});

UserSchema.virtual('fullName')
.get(function () { return `${this.firstName} ${this.lastName}`; })
.set(function (v: string) {
const [first, ...rest] = v.split(' ');
this.firstName = first;
this.lastName = rest.join(' ');
});
Virtuals aren't persisted. Include in JSON output with toJSON: { virtuals: true }.
Getters / setters transform field values on read / write:
const Schema = new Schema({
email: {
type: String,
get: (v: string) => v.toLowerCase(),
set: (v: string) => v.trim(),
},
});
Caveat: getters don't run inside .lean() calls. Setters run on assignment + save.

// instance method
UserSchema.methods.greet = function () { return `Hi, ${this.name}`; };
const u = await User.findById(id);
u.greet();
// static (collection-level)
UserSchema.statics.findByEmail = function (email: string) {
return this.findOne({ email });
};
await User.findByEmail('ada@x');
// chainable query helper
UserSchema.query.active = function () { return this.where({ deletedAt: null }); };
await User.find().active().sort({ createdAt: -1 });
Keep models tidy by attaching domain logic here.

const PostSchema = new Schema({
title: String,
author: { type: Schema.Types.ObjectId, ref: 'User' },
comments: [{ type: Schema.Types.ObjectId, ref: 'Comment' }],
});
const post = await Post.findById(id)
.populate('author') // fetch author doc
.populate({ path: 'comments', select: 'text createdAt' }); // fetch comments
Multi-step:
await Post.findById(id).populate({
path: 'comments',
populate: { path: 'author', select: 'name' }
});
Conditional populate:
await Post.find().populate({
path: 'comments',
match: { hidden: false },
options: { sort: { createdAt: -1 }, limit: 10 },
});

Single collection holds multiple subtypes:
const EventSchema = new Schema(
{ user: String, at: Date },
{ discriminatorKey: 'kind', collection: 'events' }
);
const Event = model('Event', EventSchema);
const ClickEvent = Event.discriminator('Click', new Schema({
url: String,
button: String,
}));
const PurchaseEvent = Event.discriminator('Purchase', new Schema({
amount: Number,
items: [String],
}));
await ClickEvent.create({ user: 'ada', at: new Date(), url: '/home', button: 'cta' });
await PurchaseEvent.create({ user: 'ada', at: new Date(), amount: 50, items: ['p1'] });
await Event.find(); // all events of all types
await ClickEvent.find(); // only clicks
The discriminator key kind is stored on each doc (Click or Purchase). Queries on the subtype model auto-filter.
Use for the polymorphic pattern (Post 07). Cleaner than manual type field handling in the app.
Embedded discriminators work too — array of mixed-type sub-documents.

Plugins add cross-cutting schema features. Pattern:
function softDelete(schema: Schema) {
schema.add({ deletedAt: { type: Date, default: null } });
schema.pre(/^find/, function (this: any) { this.where({ deletedAt: null }); });
schema.methods.softDelete = function () {
this.deletedAt = new Date();
return this.save();
};
}
UserSchema.plugin(softDelete);
PostSchema.plugin(softDelete);

const session = await mongoose.startSession();
try {
await session.withTransaction(async () => {
const acc = await Account.findById('A').session(session);
if (acc.balance < amount) throw new Error('insufficient');
acc.balance -= amount;
await acc.save({ session });
await Account.updateOne({ _id: 'B' }, { $inc: { balance: amount } }, { session });
await Transfer.create([{ from: 'A', to: 'B', amount }], { session });
});
} finally {
await session.endSession();
}
Pass { session } to every Mongoose op inside. Same rules + retry semantics as the official-driver pattern (Post 09).
Note Model.create([...], { session }) — array form is required when passing options to create.

Schema-level:
UserSchema.index({ email: 1 }, { unique: true });
UserSchema.index({ createdAt: -1 });
UserSchema.index({ name: 'text', bio: 'text' });
On startup, Mongoose calls createIndexes for each model. In production, disable auto-indexing + create indexes through migrations:
mongoose.set('autoIndex', process.env.NODE_ENV !== 'production');
Auto-indexing blocks reads/writes on first run with a big collection. Always control index builds explicitly in production.

const AddressSchema = new Schema({
line1: String,
city: String,
}, { _id: false }); // no _id on each subdoc
const UserSchema = new Schema({
name: String,
addresses: [AddressSchema],
});
const u = await User.findById(id);
u.addresses.push({ line1: '...', city: 'London' });
u.addresses.id(subdocId).remove(); // pull by subdoc _id
await u.save();
Subdocument arrays are validated + middleware-aware. Use them for bounded 1:few children (Post 07).

When a field genuinely has dynamic shape:
const Schema = new Schema({
payload: Schema.Types.Mixed,
});
const doc = new Model({ payload: {} });
doc.payload.foo = 'bar';
doc.markModified('payload'); // required! Mongoose can't detect nested Mixed changes
await doc.save();
For SaaS multi-tenant where each tenant has custom fields, prefer the attribute pattern (Post 07) over Mixed.
